%T A397774 6,49,4,51,2,55,4,63,8,69,6,77,8,75,2,75,0,3,2,9,6,9,0,5,2,9,4,9,0,7,
%U A397774 2,9,2,15,6,9,6,25,16,21,4,15,6,35,20,51,16,33,18,55
%N A397774 Irregular triangle where the n-th row is the sequence b_n(m) defined in comments.
%C A397774 Define a sequence b_n(m) = b(m) as follows with the n understood: b(1) = 2*n-1, b(2) = 2, b(m) = abs(b(m-1)-b(m-3)) for odd m, and b(m) = b(m-1) + p for even m where p is the least unused prime such that b(m-1) + p is an odd composite.
%C A397774 It is conjectured that b_n(m) reaches 0 for every n, at which point the sequence b_n(m) terminates because 0+p can never be composite. The index at which 0 occurs must necessarily be even.
%C A397774 If this conjecture is false, then eventually we will encounter some row that does not terminate and the total number of rows in this sequence would then be finite, but the number of terms infinite.
%C A397774 b_n(m) terminates for all n <= 10000. - _Sean A. Irvine_, Jul 23 2026
%e A397774 For row 1 b_1(1) = b(1)=1, b_1(2) = b(2)=2. For subsequent odd terms, the lowest unused prime p is used so that b(2)+p yields a composite. Reject 2+3, 2+5 but accept 7, giving b(3) = 2+7 = 9. For subsequent even terms, b(4) is the absolute difference of the prior two odd terms, thus = abs(9-1) = 8. The lowest unused prime p so that b(4)+p is an odd composite is now 13, giving b(5) = 8+13 = 21. The final row 1 build is 1, 2, 9, 8, 21, 12, 15, 6, 25, 10, 15, 10, 21, 6, 35, 14, 45, 10, 27, 18, 55, 28, 51, 4, 45, 6, 49, 4, 51, 2, 55, 4, 63, 8, 69, 6, 77, 8, 75, 2, 75, 0. For row 2 the build is 3, 2, 9, 6, 9, 0. And so on.
%Y A397774 Cf. A397755 (row lengths).
%K A397774 nonn,tabf,new
%O A397774 1,2
%A A397774 _Bill McEachen_, Jul 09 2026
